6. PARQUET
----------
- open source columnar file format.
- designed for efficient storage & analysis on big data
- developed by twitter & now an Apache project. 
- works with any data processing engine.
- optimized for analytics, not for fast row level writes.
- often used with partitioning in folder structure to speed up queries.
- use appropriate row group size ( 128 MB - 512 MB)
- choose the right compression code (snappy)


PARQUET FILE STRUCTURE
-----------------------
FOOTER 
ROW GROUP 1
ROW GROUP 2
...........
ROW GROUP 3

MAGIC
(PAR1)

FOOTER 
- contains metadata & schema
- points to all row groups

ROW GROUP 
- horizontal partition of data
- default size : 12b MB

COLUMN CHUNKS ( inside row group)
- each col stored seperately
- encoded & compressed

MAGIC NUMBER
- identifies file as parquet(PAR1)

WHY COLUMANR ? 
- better performance & better compression (similar values stored together)
- read only required cols
- less i/o
- faster aggregation
- ideal for analytics

KEY FEATURES
COLUMNAR STORAGE - read only what you need
compression - snappy, gzip, zstd
SCHEMA SUPPORT - self describing ( in footer)
EFFICIENT ENCODING - dictionary, Delta
SPLITTABLE - enables ||le processing
NESTED DATA SUPPORT - arrays, maps, structs
CROSS PLATFORM - lang & engine agnostic.

ADV OF PARQUET
- high performance for analysis
- reduced storage cost.
- scalable for big data
- works with many engines
- self describing & schema evolution friendly

LIMITATIONS
- not efficient for row level updates.
- schema changes require rewriting data
- not suitable for small files (overhead)





6. DELTA TABLES
----------------------------------
* yes, Delta tables are built on top of parquet files, when reading a delta table, you are actually reading immutable parqurt
files containing the data, managed by a JSON based transaction log (_delta_log) that ensures ACID transactions,
schema enforcements & time travel. 

Key details on Parquet & delta:
-------------------------------
Storage format -> The actual data is stored in snappy compressed parquet files.
Delta log -> A delta table includes a _delta-log / directory containing JSON files that tracks additions
& removal of parquet files. 

Difference -> Parquet is just the storage format, delta lake is an optimization / governance layer over those parquet files.

Summary -> Delta table files are essentially parquet files + transaction logs. 
---------


DELTA TABLE -- Parquet file + trans log
- is a table format / storage layer built on top of parquet.
- it stores data with additional capabilities likee
- ACID transaction
- Efficient file rewrites
- time travel support
- Optimized deletes.
- schema evolution
- upserts (Merge)
- deletes & updates

DELTA LIVE TABLES (DLT)
- is a managed ETL/ELT pipeline framework in databricks
- instead of manually managing ETL jobs, DLT :
    - builds pipeline
    - orchestrate dependencies
    - handles failures
    - monitor jobs
    - validates DQ
    - automates batch / streaming execution
- in DLT, o/p tables are generally delta tables underneath. 
- DLT internally creates & manages delta tables
- delta table -- data storage
- DLT - data pipeline automation

DLT vs TRADITIONAL SPARK NOTEBOOK
DLT
-when I need managed, reliable, production-grade ETL with buiil-in monitoring, lineage & DQ.
- especially for bronze/silver PL or streaming workloads.
TRADITIONAL SPARK NOTEBOOK
- when the logic is highly customised, requires external integration / fine grained perf tuning.
- hybrid approach works best
- spark notebook - manual driving -> more control, more flexibility
- DLT - Automatic car with built-in safety features -> less manual effort, easier maintenance. 


